From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 17:10:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] A cut-out is not a translucent surface

Patch 0010 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

Patch 0006 gave every draw in the microcode's translucent z mode a query mask that no ray
carries, so that a flame's billboard does not shadow its own torch. That rule is right for a
billboard and wrong for a CUT-OUT: a draw whose texels are either written whole or discarded is
opaque wherever it is drawn, whatever its z mode says, and this game hangs its trees, vines,
ladders and fences on exactly that.

The consequence had three faces, which is why it was reported three times as three things. Such
a surface was traced by nothing, so it took no light at any distance and no shadow. It was
sorted into the raster scene that runs AFTER the composite, so it took no fog share and no
relight. And being drawn after the composite, it covered whatever had been drawn in front of it,
which is why a fairy's glow disappeared behind a tree she was standing in front of.

The user, 2026-09-30: "trees still do not allow navi or light or fog in front of it", with the
note that the same shape of fault had been fixed before on dark doorways and on the sun's
shadows over a glow. It had, and both of those were the same thing: a draw landing on the wrong
side of the composite.

A cut-out is now recognized by the coverage cut or the alpha compare, the two ways the microcode
says "whole or not at all", and takes the depth mask like any wall. The primary ray sees it so it
takes light, the shadow rays see it so it casts one, and it rasterizes before the composite so it
takes the fog and the relight with the surface it hangs on. Its holes are the alpha test at the
hit, as they already are for every cut-out the game draws in the opaque z mode.

The Experiment row's "Cut-outs translucent" puts patch 0006's rule back, so the two can be put
side by side the way the last several lighting questions were settled.

With the ray tracing row Off none of this code runs: the classification sits inside the traced
projection's branch, so the Off-is-identical rule is untouched.
---
diff --git a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
--- a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
+++ b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
@@ -1782,8 +1782,40 @@ namespace RT64 {
                         raytracing.cullDisable = !call.shaderDesc.flags.culling;
 
                         const interop::OtherMode &otherMode = call.callDesc.otherMode;
+                        // A CUT-OUT IS NOT A TRANSLUCENT SURFACE (patch 0010). A draw whose
+                        // texels are either drawn whole or not at all, which is what the
+                        // coverage cut and the alpha compare both say, is opaque wherever it IS
+                        // drawn, however its z mode reads. This game hangs its trees, vines,
+                        // ladders and fences on that and draws them in the translucent z mode,
+                        // where patch 0006's rule gave them a mask no ray carries: traced by
+                        // nothing, so lit by nothing at any distance; and sorted into the raster
+                        // scene that runs AFTER the composite, so they take no fog share and
+                        // cover whatever was drawn in front of them.
+                        //
+                        // That is the whole of the user's report of 2026-09-30, "trees still do
+                        // not allow navi or light or fog in front of it", and of the two cases
+                        // he remembered being fixed before it (the dark doorways, the sun's
+                        // shadows over a glow): all of them are a draw landing on the wrong side
+                        // of the composite.
+                        //
+                        // With this, a cut-out takes the depth mask like any wall: the primary
+                        // ray sees it so it takes light, the shadow rays see it so it casts, and
+                        // it rasterizes BEFORE the composite so it takes the fog and the relight
+                        // with the surface it hangs on. Its holes stay the alpha test at the
+                        // hit, as they already are for every cut-out the game draws in the
+                        // opaque z mode.
+                        //
+                        // WHY THIS IS A PATCH OF ITS OWN AND NOT A CORRECTION TO 0008: the rule
+                        // it replaces is patch 0006's, the series is a history, and this is the
+                        // change that has to be easy to find again if a cut-out ever needs to go
+                        // back. The Experiment row's "Cut-outs translucent" puts 0006's rule
+                        // back for a side by side, which is how the last few lighting questions
+                        // were settled.
+                        const uint32_t acMode = otherMode.alphaCompare();
+                        const bool cutoutDraw = otherMode.cvgXAlpha() || (acMode == G_AC_THRESHOLD) || (acMode == G_AC_DITHER);
+                        const bool cutoutTranslucent = (float(rtResources->rtParams.tuneExperiment.x) == 8.0f);
                         // A translucent draw (the XLU z mode) gets a mask no ray carries (patch 0006).
-                        if (otherMode.zMode() == ZMODE_XLU) {
+                        if ((otherMode.zMode() == ZMODE_XLU) && !(cutoutDraw && !cutoutTranslucent)) {
                             raytracing.queryMask = TranslucentRayQueryMask;
                         }
                         else {
